            <ArticleTitle>Flap infection associated with medicinal leeches in reconstructive surgery: two new drug-resistant organisms.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>457-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1055/s-0033-1343956</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The use of medicinal leeches in reconstructive surgery has proven value for the salvage of flaps with venous congestion but is associated with a risk of leech-acquired infection. The most common leech-associated organism is Aeromonas hydrophila, which antibiotic prophylaxis is typically directed against. The authors describe two new multidrug-resistant organisms acquired from medicinal leech therapy that resulted in flap infection. The evaluation of suspected leech-borne infection and management protocol for this leech-acquired resistant multi-organism infection is presented.</AbstractText>
